# Why do profits disappear in a competitive market?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "How does entry compete profits away? Why is zero economic profit not zero accounting profit?"
next: {"title":"What does a monopolist do differently?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/32-what-does-a-monopolist-do-differently.cast"}

## A craze and a profit
use: firm_cost_curves
with:
    mode: competition
    show:
      - mc
      - atc
      - price_line
    price: 55
    shade: profit
    mark_minima: true
sources:
    - id: crumbs2014
      title: Crumbs closes stores nationwide
      authors: Fortune
      year: 2014
      url: https://fortune.com/2014/07/08/crumbs-closes-stores-nationwide/
      finding: Crumbs Bake Shop, founded in New York in 2003 and public since 2011, shut all its remaining stores on 7 July 2014 after Nasdaq delisted it, amid weak results and a flagging desire for cupcakes.

    icon cupcake size 150 set twemoji x 500 y 520 icon_look picture of cupcake hidden true
    text t1 "Crumbs, New York: 2003" cites ["crumbs2014"] x 500 y 390 font_size 32 hidden true
    text t2 "2011: on the stock market" cites ["crumbs2014"] x 500 y 330 font_size 32 hidden true
    text t3 "July 2014: every store shut" #b5482e cites ["crumbs2014"] x 500 y 270 font_size 32 hidden true
    text fading "craze fading" #8f887c x 500 y 110 font_size 28 hidden true
    dot mc_low at.data [30,12] hidden true
    text p_read "$5.50" #b5482e at.data [8,59] font_size 26 hidden true
    text per_loaf "Profit ≈ $2 a loaf" at.data [38,63] font_size 28 hidden true
    icon cc1 size 60 set twemoji x 250 y 170 icon_look picture of cupcake hidden true
    icon cc2 size 60 set twemoji x 350 y 170 icon_look picture of cupcake hidden true
    icon cc3 size 60 set twemoji x 450 y 170 icon_look picture of cupcake hidden true
    icon cc4 size 60 set twemoji x 550 y 170 icon_look picture of cupcake hidden true
    icon cc5 size 60 set twemoji x 650 y 170 icon_look picture of cupcake hidden true
    icon cc6 size 60 set twemoji x 750 y 170 icon_look picture of cupcake hidden true

    card title "A craze and a profit"

    hide label_shade p_star label_p_star

Why do profits disappear in a competitive market? Start with cupcakes.
    draw cupcake

B: In the 2000s, cupcake shops were the hot new business in America. Crumbs Ba (@draw t1@)ke Shop opened in New York in 2003, and by 2011 it wa (@draw t2@)s on the stock market.

In July 2014 it shut every store it had left, almost overnight.
    draw t3

B: By then cupcakes were sold n (@draw cc4 cc5 cc6 parallel true@)early everywhere, and the cra (@draw fading@)ze was fading.
    draw cc1 cc2 cc3 parallel true

    erase cupcake t1 t2 t3 fading cc1 cc2 cc3 cc4 cc5 cc6

To see the mechanism, shrink it to one baker: Mia, in Harbourtown, whose sourdough has become the town's craze.
    draw axes

Here are her costs, with illustrative numbers: the average cost of a loaf, ATC, and th (@draw mc label_mc@)e cost of one more loaf, MC.
    draw atc label_atc

B: Sourdough sells for, say, five dollars fifty a (@draw p_read@)t the Saturday market. Mia has to take that price: charge more, and the stall next door gets her customers.
    draw price_line label_price_line

She bakes up to where one more loaf would cost more than it fetches. On average ea (@draw shade@)ch loaf earns her about two dollar (@draw per_loaf@)s more than it costs.
    draw q_star label_q_star

B: That box is her profit. And nothing stops anyone else in town from renting an oven.
    highlight shade effect box

    erase per_loaf

So where does Mia's point end up once the others pile in?
    draw min_atc mc_low parallel true

    ask "Where does Mia's point end up once anyone can open a bakery? Tap one of the three dots." choose ["q_star","min_atc","mc_low"] answer min_atc
        right: The bottom of the ATC curve. Newcomers keep coming while there is any profit to chase, and that stops only where price meets the lowest average cost.
        wrong: Ask what keeps drawing new bakers in, and when it would run out. It's the bottom of ATC.

    draw label_min_atc

## Entry
use: firm_cost_curves
with:
    mode: competition
    show:
      - mc
      - atc
      - price_line
    price: 55
    shade: profit
    mark_minima: true

    icon b1 size 50 set twemoji x 640 y 215 icon_look picture of bread hidden true
    icon b2 size 50 set twemoji x 688 y 215 icon_look picture of bread hidden true
    icon b3 size 50 set twemoji x 736 y 215 icon_look picture of bread hidden true
    icon b4 size 50 set twemoji x 784 y 215 icon_look picture of bread hidden true
    icon b5 size 50 set twemoji x 832 y 215 icon_look picture of bread hidden true
    icon b6 size 50 set twemoji x 880 y 215 icon_look picture of bread hidden true
    icon b7 size 50 set twemoji x 928 y 215 icon_look picture of bread hidden true
    text r55 "$5.50" #b5482e at.data [8,59] font_size 26 hidden true
    text r45 "$4.50" #b5482e at.data [8,49] font_size 26 hidden true
    text r34 "$3.40" #b5482e at.data [8,30] font_size 28 hidden true
    text zero "Profit: zero" at.data [62,28] font_size 28 hidden true
    polygon lossbox points [[124,245.8],[486.7,245.8],[486.7,294.2],[124,294.2]] style.fill #c96567 style.stroke #c96567 hidden true
    text losslab "Loss" #c96567 x 298 y 322 font_size 28 hidden true

    card title Entry

    hide label_shade label_q_star p_star label_p_star

Mia's two dollars a loaf is a signal everyone in town can read.
    draw axes atc label_atc mc label_mc price_line label_price_line q_star shade r55

B: So I open  (@draw b1@)a bakery too. W (@draw b2@)hat does that do to Mia?

    erase r55

Every new oven adds (@draw b3 b4 parallel true@) loaves to the Saturday market, a (@animate price 45 duration 3@)nd to sell them all, the price has to fall.

    draw r45

B: Then surely people stop opening bakeries once the price drops a bit?
    highlight shade effect box

Not while any box is left (@draw b5 b6 parallel true@). At four fifty there's still profit, so more bakers come.

    erase r45

Only here, at the bottom of ATC, three forty a loaf, does the b (@draw r34 min_atc parallel true@)ox shrink to  (@draw zero@)nothing.
    animate price 34 duration 3

    erase zero shade label_shade r34 label_q_star

B: And if too  (@draw b7@)many open, and t (@animate price 26 duration 2@)he pric (@draw lossbox losslab parallel true@)e falls below that?

Then every baker loses money, some c (@erase b7@)lose, and the price climb (@animate price 34 duration 2@)s back (@erase lossbox losslab@). The bottom of ATC is where entry a (@draw label_min_atc r34@)nd exit both stop.

B: Try it: drag the price above and below three forty, and watch where Mia's point sits against her average cost.
    explore params ["price"]

So the profit doesn't just fade. It draws in the  (@highlight min_atc effect pulse@)very competition that erases it.
    erase b1 b2 b3 b4 b5 b6

## Zero is not nothing
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - 'Books: profit'
      - Wage elsewhere
      - Interest elsewhere
      - Economic profit
    values:
      - 54
      - 46
      - 8
      - 0
    value_labels: true
    y_label: $ thousand a year (illustrative)
    icons:
      - notebook
      - paintbrush
      - bank
      - null
    colors:
      - green
      - red
      - red
      - green
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 80
    label_size: 26

    text minus "minus what she gives up" #b5482e x 640 y 540 font_size 32 hidden true
    text normal "normal profit" #2e7d32 x 225 y 555 font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "Zero is not nothing"

Zero profit sounds like a disaster. Why would Mia keep baking for nothing?
    draw axes

B: Because the economist's zero and the accountant's zero are different zeros. Say Mia's bo (@draw bar_1@)oks for a year show fifty-four thousand dollars of profit.

B: That's what's left in the till after flour, rent and her assistant's wage: accounting profit.
    highlight bar_1

So where does the economist's zero come from?
    draw minus

B: From what Mia gives up. She could run the paint shop  (@draw bar_2@)at the boatyard for, say, forty-six thousand a year.

B: And the two hundred thousand she sank into ovens could earn four per cent i (@draw bar_3@)n the bank: eight thousand more.

    erase minus

    ask "What is Mia's economic profit? Drag the last bar to your guess." on bar_4 store g default "30" voice b
        intro: So what is her economic profit?
        right: Yes: {g.true}. Fifty-four, minus forty-six, minus eight. Baking pays her exactly what her time and money would earn elsewhere.
        wrong: You said {g}; it is {g.true}. Fifty-four, minus forty-six, minus eight: baking pays exactly what her time and money would earn elsewhere.

So Mia takes home fifty-four thousand at zero economic profi (@draw normal@)t. Economists call that a normal profit.

B: Those forgone costs were inside her ATC all along. Its bottom is where baking pays exactly as well as the paint shop and the bank.
    highlight bar_2 bar_3

    erase normal

That's why entry stops there: at zero economic profit, a newcomer gains nothing by joining, and Mia gains nothing by leaving.
    highlight bar_4 effect circle

## Where profits survive
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: Not a dollar of economic profit left on the table.
    good: You'd spot a normal profit on any balance sheet.
    poor: Accountants and economists both disagree with you there.
    none: The opportunity cost of skipping was, admittedly, zero.
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - About 2013
      - '2018'
    values:
      - 1
      - 0.2
    value_labels: false
    y_label: $ million, one NYC taxi medallion
    icons:
      - taxi
      - taxi
    bar_colors: same
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 1.2
    box:
      x: 90
      'y': 110
      w: 560
      h: 520
sources:
    - id: medallion
      title: Taxi medallion
      authors: Wikipedia
      year: 2024
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taxi_medallion
      finding: New York City medallion prices peaked around 2013 at over $1,000,000; by 2018 a medallion could be bought for less than $200,000, after ride-hailing competition.

    text cue "profit → licence price" #b5482e x 830 y 500 font_size 32 hidden true
    box conf1 "Free entry erodes profit:\nmostly agreed" x 830 y 500 width 300 height 120 font_size 27 hidden true
    box conf2 "Today's markets:\ncontested" #b5482e x 830 y 330 width 300 height 120 font_size 27 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"test"}] then test hidden true
    scratch math work [{"tex":"\\pi = (P - ATC)\\times Q"},{"tex":"P \\gtrless ATC:\\ \\text{in / out}"},{"tex":"P = MC = \\min ATC"}] x 500 y 430 font_size 30 hidden true
    cards costs select "In the books" hidden true
        * "Flour" in true
        * "Rent paid" in true
        * "Staff wages" in true
        * "Owner's forgone salary" in false
        * "Interest on own savings" in false
    text v1 "over $1m" cites ["medallion"] x 230 y 600 font_size 30 hidden true
    text v2 "under $0.2m" cites ["medallion"] x 510 y 378 font_size 30 hidden true
    icon truck size 160 set twemoji x 500 y 430 icon_look picture of "delivery truck" hidden true
    icon camera size 160 set twemoji x 500 y 430 icon_look picture of camera hidden true
    text closing "free entry: profit → normal profit" x 500 y 400 font_size 48 hidden true

    card title "Where profits survive"

Some profits seem to dodge entry. New York's taxis are the classic case.
    draw axes

B: The city capped the number of cabs, so the medallion, the licence to run  (@draw bar_1@)one, sold for over a million do (@draw v1@)llars around 2013.

And a driver who bought one then could expect about zero eco (@draw cue@)nomic profit: the fares mostly went to pay off the licence.

B: So the profit didn't vanish. It went into the price of the licence, to whoever got hold of one early.
    highlight bar_1

    erase cue

Then ride-hailing apps found a way round the cap, and by 2018 a me (@draw bar_2@)dallion sold for under two hundre (@draw v2@)d thousand.

B: Entry won in the end, just by a side door.
    highlight bar_2 effect circle

So how sure are we? Where entry is free,  (@draw conf1@)profits get competed away: mostly agreed.

B: Whether today's real markets st (@draw conf2@)ill work that way is contested, and that is the question of market power.

    erase conf1 conf2 axes bar_1 bar_2 v1 v2

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own
        intro: Want the math behind where entry stops?

@math

    erase mathq

Profit is P minus ATC, times Q.
    draw math_box math_line_1

Firms enter while the price is above ATC, and leave while it is below.
    draw math_line_2

Each firm sets P equal to MC, and MC cuts ATC at its bottom, so in the long run P equals MC equals the bottom of ATC.
    draw math_line_3

    erase math

@test

    erase mathq

    draw truck

    quiz "A town caps food-truck licences at ten, and they trade at $80,000 each. You buy one. What economic profit can you expect?" voice b
        intro: Three questions on new cases.
        right: About zero. The seller charged you for the profit the cap protects, so the truck just pays back what you paid for the licence.
        wrong: Ask what the seller of the licence was charging you for. It's about zero: the licence price absorbed the profit.
        * Large: the cap shields you
        + About zero: the licence price absorbed it
        * Negative: trucks always lose

    erase truck

    draw costs

    ask "Which of these costs show up in an accountant's books? Tap every one." on costs stage own store f
        right: Right: flour, rent and wages are paid out. The owner's forgone salary and interest are costs only an economist counts.
        wrong: You got {f}. Flour, rent and wages are paid out; the owner's forgone salary and interest are costs only an economist counts.

    erase costs

    draw camera

    quiz "Demand for film-camera repairs collapses, and repair shops start losing money. What happens in the long run?" voice b
        right: Some close. With fewer shops, the price of a repair recovers until the ones that stay earn a normal profit again.
        wrong: Ask what an owner who loses money every month does next. Some close, until the rest earn a normal profit.
        * Shops keep losing money for good
        + Some close, until those left earn a normal profit
        * Repairs become free

    erase camera

So why do profits disappear? Because where anyone can copy a good idea, someone w (@draw closing@)ill, until it pays no more than the owner's time and money would earn elsewhere.

